          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "57653019-24bd-5bff-9854-c1baede4a024"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186110756-draft/plots/daa50f68fdb1471280ef60302875442cfc6e818f475c5cd5bbb317a73fe65176.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{14}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{14}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{14}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{14})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{27}{196}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{169}{196}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{13}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{13}{14}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{13}{14})}=\\frac{14}{13}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    },
