      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186110756-draft/plots/913bf47eb7be05b583749fd81a9a938d3b541cd07037a366e5ef6d5f4ec3fabe.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{11}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{11}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{11}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{11})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{96}{121}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{121}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{5}{11}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{11})}=\\frac{11}{5}.\n$$",
