      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186110756-draft/plots/6b9ea9c64450840ccd9b8f1df75ac46307b204aa2447e6c53568db3cb976bd4a.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{12}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{12}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{12})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{144}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{133}{144}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{133}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{133}}{12}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{133}}{12})}=\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{133}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{133}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{133}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{133}}{\\sqrt{133}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{133}}{133}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
