      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186110756-draft/plots/63cba8803ac5cbd1faf1d64776995ad6f8bbaee153e5bcdd0bf2d39876fc5640.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{93}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{93}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{93}}{10}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{93}}{10})}=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{93}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{93}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{93}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{93}}{\\sqrt{93}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{93}}{93} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{93}}{93}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
